{"entries":{"chapter-43-study-falls|Chapter 43 Study: Falls":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"epidemiology|Epidemiology":["u3"],"copy|Fall = event resulting in a person coming to rest inadvertently on the ground/floor/lower level (p633).":["u4"],"copy|Community-dwelling 65+: fall incidence 32–40%; beyond 75: fall rates 40–50% (prospective studies; no time frame stated) (p633).":["u5"],"copy|Residential aged care (RACF) residents: fall incidence 30–56% (p633).":["u6"],"copy|Hospital falls: 2% (general hospitals) up to 27% (acute geriatric wards) (p633).":["u7"],"copy|37–56% of falls cause minor injury; 10–15% cause major injury (p633).":["u8"],"copy|Falls = 14% of ED admissions and 4% of all hospital admissions in 65+ (p633).":["u9"],"copy|Falls without injury still matter: fear of falling → reduced mobility/frailty and a key driver of institutionalization (p633).":["u10"],"risk-factors|Risk Factors":["u11"],"copy|Demographic: advancing age and female gender in the community; in hospital/institutional settings risk is similar or even higher in men (p634).":["u12"],"copy|Alcohol has NOT been shown to be a fall-risk factor in large cohort studies (despite causing osteoporosis/cerebellar atrophy/neuropathy) — likely underreporting/selection bias (p634).":["u13"],"copy|Medical: stroke, Parkinson disease, MS, dementia, depression, dizziness, incontinence, arthritis. Incontinence and cognitive impairment matter more in institutions; orthostatic hypotension and vestibular disorders aren't strong community risk factors but matter in specific populations (e.g., orthostatic hypotension in Parkinson disease) (p634).":["u14"],"copy|Medications: psychoactive drugs (benzodiazepine, antidepressant, antipsychotic) → 2–3x fall risk, ~2x hip-fracture risk (p634). NSAIDs: most recent systematic review found no significant increase on adjusted analysis (p636). Some antihypertensive classes (angiotensin system blocking medications) are protective; fall risk is significantly increased within the 24 hours after starting an antihypertensive or changing its dose (p636).":["u15"],"copy|Balance/gait: slower speed, shorter steps, wider step width, more double-support time predict falls; poor trunk/head control while walking impairs gaze stability (p636).":["u16"],"copy|Sensory/neuromuscular: reduced contrast sensitivity/depth perception and reduced knee-extension/ankle-dorsiflexor strength (especially in RACF) are risk factors (p636).":["u17"],"copy|Neuropsychological: poor dual-task performance (e.g., talking while walking), poor executive function/attention, fear of falling and depression → inactivity/isolation (p636).":["u18"],"copy|Environmental: it has been estimated that extrinsic factors are involved in 35–45% of injurious falls; 1/4 to 1/2 of falls occur away from home; a stairs fall carries ~2x the injury risk of other falls (p636–637).":["u19"],"copy|Table 43-1 rates each risk factor's association with falls on a */**/*** scale; use of ≥4 medications carries the same top rating (***) as psychoactive-drug use, and living alone/inactivity are rated ** (Table 43-1, p635).":["u20"],"screening-amp-assessment|Screening & Assessment":["u21"],"copy|Ask about falls in the last 12 months + assess balance/gait — previous falls is one of the strongest predictors of a future fall (p637).":["u22"],"copy|TUG test: rise from chair, walk 3 meters, turn, return, sit; a 25-study systematic review found limited predictive value in community-dwelling older adults and no cut-point for impaired performance could be recommended (p637).":["u23"],"copy|Alternatives with good predictive accuracy: Alternate Step Test, Sit-to-Stand Test, Short Physical Performance Battery (each domain scored 0–4, max total 12) (p637).":["u24"],"copy|Hospital (UK NICE): automatically assess all patients 65+, or 50–64 with a risk condition (e.g., recent stroke) (p637).":["u25"],"copy|RACF: CaHFRiS screen items — MMSE < 17, impulsivity, reduced standing balance, walking-frame use, a fall in the past year, antidepressant/hypnotic-anxiolytic use; absolute 6-month fall risk ranges 0% (no factors) → 100% (≥6 factors) (p637).":["u26"],"prevention-single-interventions|Prevention — Single Interventions":["u27"],"copy|Exercise: 23% fewer falls across 64 RCTs (community-dwelling); best results with high balance challenge + ≥3 hrs/week of prescribed exercise, effective even in 75+ (p638). Rapid-stepping training: 52% fewer falls (7-RCT meta-analysis) (p638). CAUTION — not a universal win: not effective in long-term stroke survivors or people just discharged from hospital; one home-based program increased falls by 43% in the recently-discharged (p639). In contrast: RACF resistance + balance training (25 wks) cut falls 55% (p639); a multifaceted RACF program (staff training/feedback + resident education + environmental adaptations + hip protectors + 2x/week group exercise) cut falls 45% (p639).":["u28"],"copy|Vision: first-eye cataract surgery → 34% fewer falls; second-eye surgery similar (~32%) but not statistically significant; one prospective cohort study associated cataract surgery with fewer falls provided post-op spectacle lens power updates were cautious (less than ±0.75 diopter change) (p639). Updating glasses to correct prescription: evidence less clear — one RCT found no significant reduction, and another (vision assessment + treatment) increased falls by ~50% (p639). Single-lens distance glasses plus counseling about ground-level hazards for habitual multifocal wearers: 40% fewer falls in the outdoor-active subgroup (p639). Home-safety modification for severe uncorrectable low vision: 41% fewer falls (p639).":["u29"],"copy|Medications: withdrawing centrally-acting drugs did NOT significantly reduce falls in the most recent systematic review (5 trials, 1309 participants; RaR 0.98, 95% CI 0.63–1.51) — better to prevent uptake than deprescribe later (p639). Avoid prescribing benzodiazepines/z-drugs/other psychotropics for insomnia unless there is a clear pattern of addiction or inability to complete a withdrawal program; consider nonpharmacological approaches (p639). Vitamin D: no benefit in community-dwelling older adults (even if deficient); may reduce falls in RACF residents (p639); Required reading: see AGS Beers 2023 required-article card for anticholinergics with prior falls or fractures (Table 3, p2066)":["u30"],"copy|Home safety: OT-delivered intensive interventions cut fall risk 21% overall, 39% in high-risk groups (p640); a cluster RCT of 842 households found home modifications cut fall-injury rate 26% overall and 39% for intervention-specific injuries (p640).":["u31"],"copy|Footwear/podiatry: multifaceted podiatry → 23% fewer falls; multifactorial interventions incorporating podiatry → 27% fewer falls (community) (p640); a RACF podiatry pilot was protective at 3 months but the effect was gone by 6 months (p641).":["u32"],"copy|Psychological: most CBT programs used alone have not prevented falls in people with fear of falling; adding CBT to multifactorial/exercise programs may help (p641).":["u33"],"copy|Cardiovascular: pacemaker implantation for cardioinhibitory carotid sinus hypersensitivity reduces drop attacks, syncope, and fall frequency (p641).":["u34"],"prevention-multifactorial|Prevention — Multifactorial":["u35"],"copy|Cochrane review of 43 community trials: multifactorial interventions → 23% fewer falls overall (p641).":["u36"],"copy|RACF: single-component interventions generally ineffective; multidisciplinary multifactorial programs with staff education work (p641).":["u37"],"copy|Two large primary-care trials found no significant reduction in their respective primary outcomes: fractures in PreFIT (9,803 participants) and serious fall injuries in STRIDE (5,451 participants). Direct delivery and uptake of the intervention may matter when implementing prevention through existing services (p641; PreFIT, STRIDE).":["u38"],"copy|CHAPTER 43s / HAZZARD":["u39"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 43 — Falls; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u40"],"copy|Hazzard reports no significant reduction in fractures (n = 5,451) or serious fall injuries (n = 9,803) in two primary-care trials (p641). [sic — the primary reports give the reverse enrollment figures: PreFIT, 9,803 participants (fractures); STRIDE, 5,451 (serious fall injuries).] Direct delivery and uptake of the intervention may matter when implementing prevention through existing services. PreFIT · STRIDE.":["u38"],"copy|CHAPTER 43 / HAZZARD":["u39"],"falls|Falls":["u126"],"risk-factors|Risk factors":["u127"],"which-risk-factors-carry-the-most-weight|Which risk factors carry the most weight?":["u51"],"medication-history-after-a-fall|Medication history after a fall":["u52"],"vision-cataract-and-the-lower-part-of-a-multifocal-lens|Vision, cataract and the lower part of a multifocal lens":["u54"],"screening-and-mobility-assessment|Screening and mobility assessment":["u128"],"preventing-falls|Preventing falls":["u129"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u130"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u56"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u131"],"copy|Reconstruct the fall, then assess mobility, medicines, vision and the environment.":["u132"],"copy|The intervention depends on the risk factor and the setting.":["u133"],"copy|Table 43-1 grades how consistently a factor predicted falls in prospective studies.":["u57"],"copy|Dementia, stroke, advanced age, reduced peripheral sensation, depression, psychoactive medication and use of four or more drugs have strong evidence.":["u58"],"copy|Living alone has moderate evidence and may partly reflect age and sex.":["u59"],"copy|Orthostatic hypotension has a weaker association in community cohorts.":["u60"],"copy|A symptomatic postural pressure drop still deserves attention in an individual who falls when standing (p634–635).":["u61"],"copy|Medical: stroke, Parkinson disease, MS, dementia, depression, dizziness, incontinence, arthritis.":["u134"],"copy|Incontinence and cognitive impairment matter more in institutions; orthostatic hypotension and vestibular disorders aren't strong community risk factors but matter in specific populations (e.g., orthostatic hypotension in Parkinson disease) (p634).":["u135"],"copy|Consider a man with dementia, diabetes and alcohol use who takes two medicines.":["u136"],"copy|Dementia itself belongs in the strongest group.":["u63"],"copy|Diabetes becomes especially relevant when examination reveals reduced peripheral sensation.":["u64"],"copy|Cohort studies did not show a consistent association with alcohol consumption, although underreporting and selection could obscure an effect.":["u65"],"copy|A patient may have peripheral neuropathy, depression, five medicines including duloxetine, and a carotid bruit.":["u137"],"copy|The first three correspond to strongly supported risks.":["u67"],"copy|A bruit suggests arterial disease and does not identify the cardioinhibitory carotid sinus syndrome that can cause syncope (p634–635, 641).":["u68"],"copy|Looking at the feet substitutes for position information lost with advanced neuropathy.":["u138"],"copy|Looking away removes that visual compensation and the patient loses balance.":["u70"],"copy|A motor stroke would more often produce an asymmetric deficit.":["u71"],"copy|Vitamin B12 deficiency can also disturb position sense (p635–636, 670).":["u139"],"copy|Ask what the patient actually took and when anything changed.":["u73"],"copy|Escitalopram is the antidepressant among aspirin, a longstanding angiotensin receptor blocker and mirabegron.":["u140"],"copy|Antidepressants, antipsychotics and benzodiazepines are the classes consistently associated with falls in the chapter.":["u75"],"copy|The first day after an antihypertensive is started or its dose changed is a distinct risk period.":["u76"],"copy|A chronic stable prescription carries a different history (p634–636).":["u77"],"copy|Medications: psychoactive drugs (benzodiazepine, antidepressant, antipsychotic) → 2–3x fall risk, ~2x hip-fracture risk (p634).":["u141"],"copy|NSAIDs: most recent systematic review found no significant increase on adjusted analysis (p636).":["u142"],"copy|Some antihypertensive classes (angiotensin system blocking medications) are protective; fall risk is significantly increased within the 24 hours after starting an antihypertensive or changing its dose (p636).":["u143"],"copy|An older man fell while getting up to use the bathroom.":["u144"],"copy|Review sedative exposure, pulse-slowing drugs, diuresis, nocturia and orthostatic symptoms.":["u79"],"copy|Apixaban chiefly changes the consequences of trauma through bleeding.":["u80"],"copy|A medication review can identify a reversible contributor even though trials of withdrawing fall-risk drugs alone did not consistently prevent later falls (p634–636, 639).":["u81"],"copy|Contrast sensitivity is the ability to see an edge when its brightness differs little from the background.":["u93"],"copy|A person may read high-contrast letters yet miss a shallow step.":["u94"],"copy|Among visual deficits, reduced contrast sensitivity has the strongest evidence.":["u145"],"copy|Impaired depth perception can also cause misjudgment of an obstacle (p635–636).":["u96"],"copy|A patient with cataract may repeatedly trip on stairs.":["u146"],"copy|The first-eye trial found 34% fewer falls with expedited surgery.":["u98"],"copy|A similar estimate after second-eye surgery was statistically uncertain.":["u99"],"copy|The demonstrated benefit applied to outdoor-active habitual multifocal wearers who received single-lens distance glasses and counseling.":["u101"],"copy|Bifocal lower segments can blur the ground just where a stair edge must be located.":["u102"],"copy|General prescription updates had inconsistent results (pp638, 641; trial details p639 and Figures 43-3 and 43-4, p640).":["u147"],"copy|Watch the person rise, walk three metres at their usual pace with their usual aid, turn, return and sit.":["u82"],"copy|Record the quality of the transfers and gait as well as the time.":["u83"],"copy|Distinguish this fall-risk screen from scales for ADL assistance, rehabilitation function and frailty.":["u148"],"copy|A slow rise and uncertainty about previous falls warrant further assessment.":["u149"],"copy|A review of 25 community studies found limited prediction and could not recommend a time cutoff for the TUG (p637, Table 43-2).":["u86"],"copy|The Alternate Step Test requires rapid weight transfer while the other foot moves onto an 18-cm step.":["u87"],"copy|More than ten seconds for eight alternating placements indicates impairment.":["u88"],"copy|A time of 17 seconds therefore identifies a specific balance problem.":["u150"],"copy|A T-score of −2.7 describes skeletal fragility and changes the likely injury from a fall.":["u90"],"copy|Five chair rises in more than 12 seconds indicate impaired performance on a different test.":["u91"],"copy|The Short Physical Performance Battery combines standing balance, chair rises and gait, up to 12 points (p637, Table 43-2).":["u92"],"copy|For a community-dwelling patient, choose an intervention shown to prevent falls.":["u151"],"copy|Exercises that challenge balance train the tasks that avert a fall, including shifting weight, turning and taking a recovery step.":["u105"],"copy|Across 64 randomized trials there were 23% fewer falls with well-designed programs.":["u106"],"copy|Higher balance challenge and at least three prescribed hours per week were associated with larger effects.":["u107"],"copy|Individual and group delivery can both work.":["u108"],"copy|Routine vitamin D for community fall prevention and a blanket switch to multifocals do not share this evidence (p638–639).":["u109"],"copy|Medications: withdrawing centrally-acting drugs did NOT significantly reduce falls in the most recent systematic review (5 trials, 1309 participants; RaR 0.98, 95% CI 0.63–1.51) — better to prevent uptake than deprescribe later (p639).":["u152"],"copy|Avoid prescribing benzodiazepines/z-drugs/other psychotropics for insomnia unless there is a clear pattern of addiction or inability to complete a withdrawal program; consider nonpharmacological approaches (p639).":["u153"],"copy|Vitamin D: no benefit in community-dwelling older adults (even if deficient); may reduce falls in RACF residents (p639); Required reading: see AGS Beers 2023 required-article card for anticholinergics with prior falls or fractures (Table 3, p2066)":["u154"],"copy|A woman who trips on a rug at night and fractures her wrist needs a home hazard assessment.":["u155"],"copy|Remove obstacles such as low furniture and loose rugs.":["u156"],"copy|An occupational therapist observes how the person moves through the home, agrees priorities with them and follows up the adaptations.":["u112"],"copy|Safer movement matters outside the home too.":["u113"],"copy|Intensive programs reduced falls by 21% overall and by 39% in high-risk groups.":["u114"],"copy|Advice alone misses implementation and practice (p639–640).":["u115"],"copy|Vision: first-eye cataract surgery → 34% fewer falls; second-eye surgery similar (~32%) but not statistically significant; one prospective cohort study associated cataract surgery with fewer falls provided post-op spectacle lens power updates were cautious (less than ±0.75 diopter change) (p639).":["u157"],"copy|Updating glasses to correct prescription: evidence less clear — one RCT found no significant reduction, and another (vision assessment + treatment) increased falls by ~50% (p639).":["u158"],"copy|Single-lens distance glasses plus counseling about ground-level hazards for habitual multifocal wearers: 40% fewer falls in the outdoor-active subgroup (p639).":["u159"],"copy|Home-safety modification for severe uncorrectable low vision: 41% fewer falls (p639).":["u160"],"copy|A program that works for an active person in the community may expose a recently discharged patient to tasks beyond current ability.":["u116"],"copy|One home program after discharge increased falls, while a supervised program combining resistance and balance training reduced them in long-term care.":["u117"],"copy|Decide which deficits to train, what supervision is feasible and how the patient manages between sessions (p638–639).":["u118"],"copy|Exercise: 23% fewer falls across 64 RCTs (community-dwelling); best results with high balance challenge + ≥3 hrs/week of prescribed exercise, effective even in 75+ (p638).":["u161"],"copy|Rapid-stepping training: 52% fewer falls (7-RCT meta-analysis) (p638).":["u162"],"copy|CAUTION — not a universal win: not effective in long-term stroke survivors or people just discharged from hospital; one home-based program increased falls by 43% in the recently-discharged (p639).":["u163"],"copy|In contrast: RACF resistance + balance training (25 wks) cut falls 55% (p639); a multifaceted RACF program (staff training/feedback + resident education + environmental adaptations + hip protectors + 2x/week group exercise) cut falls 45% (p639).":["u164"],"copy|Hazzard reports no significant reduction in fractures (n = 5,451) or serious fall injuries (n = 9,803) in two primary-care trials (p641). [sic — the primary reports give the reverse enrollment figures: PreFIT, 9,803 participants (fractures); STRIDE, 5,451 (serious fall injuries).]":["u165"],"copy|Direct delivery and uptake of the intervention may matter when implementing prevention through existing services.":["u166"],"copy|PreFIT · STRIDE.":["u167"],"copy|משרד הבריאות השיק את התוכנית הלאומית למניעת נפילות בשנת 2017.":["u119"],"copy|בקהילה האיתור מתחיל בבירור על נפילות קודמות, קושי בהליכה והפרעה בשיווי המשקל.":["u120"],"copy|התוכנית כוללת בירור גורמי סיכון אישיים ובהם טיפול תרופתי, ביקורי בית לבדיקת בטיחות ושירותי שיקום לאחר נפילה.":["u121"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, ״התוכנית הלאומית למניעת נפילות״, סעיף ״יישום התכנית למניעת נפילות״, 20.11.2025":["u122"],"copy|אצל מי שנפל בדרך לשירותים בלילה, בודקים את המסלול מחדר השינה: מפנים רהיטים וחוטים, מסירים שטיחים או מקבעים אותם, ודואגים לתאורת לילה ולמנורה בהישג יד ליד המיטה.":["u123"],"copy|בחדר הרחצה אפשר להיעזר במרפאה בעיסוק או בפיזיותרפיסט לבחירת המיקום של ידיות האחיזה במקלחון ולצד האסלה.":["u124"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, ״סביבה בטוחה בבית למניעת נפילות״, סעיפי חדר הרחצה, הסלון וחדרי השינה והתאורה, 20.11.2025":["u125"],"copy|2020 Q30 Age and fall risk p634":["u168"],"copy|2020 Q99 Medicines associated with falls p634":["u169"],"copy|December 2021 Q94 Comparing fall risk factors p634":["u170"],"copy|June 2025 Q42 SSRIs and falls p634":["u171"],"copy|December 2021 Q61 Neuropathic gait p635":["u172"],"copy|June 2023 Q3 Dementia as a risk factor p635":["u173"],"copy|May 2024 Q93 Evidence for fall predictors p635":["u174"],"copy|December 2021 Q63 Vision treatment after falls p636":["u175"],"copy|June 2023 Q6 Contrast sensitivity p636":["u176"],"copy|June 2023 Q69 Mobility testing p637":["u177"],"copy|September 2024 Q19 Timed alternate stepping p637":["u178"],"copy|June 2026 Q2 Interpreting Timed Up and Go p637":["u179"],"copy|June 2022 Q35 Occupational home assessment p639":["u180"],"copy|June 2023 Q49 Community exercise programs p638":["u181"],"copy|June 2025 Q17 Cataract surgery p638":["u182"],"copy|September 2024 Q1 Removing home hazards p639":["u183"]},"maxId":183,"sequenceOffsets":[[190,-15]]}
